149ff4521103fd15c7187bc1a58595ab20f28be92be0f79385aee61.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the line shown in the figure above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290404 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "903d4e8d-5aeb-56dc-9b7a-c2b3d8e18a20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290404 #edb/ref 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,1,4)$ and $Q(3,-2,2).$ Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ -3t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1+4t \\\\ 1-3t \\\\ 4-2t\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+4t \\\\ y=1-3t \\\\ z=4-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186289895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290483 #edb/ref 1069 "q-236988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290483 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290483 #edb/ref 1071 "Fill in the missing parts in the parametric equations below that describe all the points on the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ where $P(6,2,1)$ and $Q(4,10,2).$\n\n| $x=$ | $6-2t$ | $t\\in [0,$ {{field-1}} $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=$ | {{field-2}} |  |\n| $z=$ | $1+t$ |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2d5d8fc-9cc6-565a-9a90-d1ac2885d17a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290483 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the line passing through the given points. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ 8t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6-2t \\\\ 2+8t \\\\ 1+t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo get the equations for the line segment, we take only $t\\in [0,1].$ Notice that substitution of $t=0$ in the above equation gives the coordinates of $P$ and substitution of $t=1$ gives the coordinates of $Q.$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the line segment $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=6-2t \\\\ y=2+8t \\\\ z=1+t\\end{aligned}\\,\\,t\\in [0,1]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336147 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336147 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336147 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c314c11-ff96-52aa-9c41-1e5203e845b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336406 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336406